<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Occitania</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Occitania is the historical region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, with parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, where the Occitan language was spoken. In the twelfth and early thirteenth centuries the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> and kings of Aragon held lands and influence there, which ended with the Albigensian Crusade and the Treaty of Corbeil of 1258.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/occitania.jpg" alt="Occitania" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Luc Viatour · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gordes_Vaucluse_France_Luc_Viatour.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
